questions.description:
```verilog
// a = 1, b = 2 before the edge
always @(posedge clk) begin
  a = b;
  b = a;
 end
```

Both assignments use the blocking operator `=`, which executes top-to-bottom **within the same time step**, updating the variable immediately — so the second line sees whatever the first line just wrote.

Starting from `a = 1, b = 2`, what are `a` and `b` immediately after one `posedge clk`?

Submit as `CTF{a_b}`.

```verilog
// a = 1, b = 2 before the first edge
always @(posedge clk) begin
  a <= b;
  b <= a;
 end
```

Both assignments now use the **non-blocking** operator `<=`. Every right-hand side in the block is evaluated using the values `a` and `b` held *before* the edge, and all the updates are applied together at the end of the time step — which is what actually makes a clean swap possible in hardware.

Give `a` and `b` immediately after the **first** `posedge clk`, and again after the **second** `posedge clk`.

Submit as `CTF{a1_b1_a2_b2}` — values after edge 1, then after edge 2.

```verilog
casez (in)
  4'b1???: out = 3;
  4'b01??: out = 2;
  4'b001?: out = 1;
  4'b0001: out = 0;
  default: out = 7;
endcase
```

`casez` treats `?` as a don't-care, and Verilog case statements match the **first** item that fits, top to bottom — later items are never even checked once an earlier one matches.

What does `out` evaluate to when `in = 4'b0110`?

Submit as `CTF{out}`.
